year to year.","summary_threshold":50,"title":"Provider Reimbursement Stability Act of 2026","viability":29.9,"viability_status":"active"},"date":"2026-07-30","detail":"Related bill","id":"119-s-5180","label":"S. 5180","title":"Provider Reimbursement Stability Act of 2026","url":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/senate-bill/5180"},{"card":{"analysis_status":"analyzed","bipartisan_band":"strong","chamber":"House","cosponsor_count":69,"id":"119-hr-9693","importance":85,"importance_band":"sweeping","introduced_date":"2026-07-15","is_demo":false,"label":"H.R. 9693","latest_action":{"date":"2026-07-15","text":"Referred to the Committee on Energy and Commerce, and in addition to the Committee on Ways and Means, for a period to be subsequently determined by the Speaker, in each case for consideration of such provisions as fall within the jurisdiction of the committee concerned."},"policy_area":"Health","sponsor":{"chamber":"House","district":"13","id":"J000302","name":"Rep. Joyce, John","party":"R","state":"PA"},"stage":"in_committee","stage_label":"Referred to committee","summary":"H.R. 9693, titled the Patients First Act of 2026, makes multiple changes to how physicians and health care practitioners are paid under the Medicare program. The bill creates a temporary hybrid payment model for certain primary care services, renames and restructures the Merit-based Incentive Payment System (MIPS) into the Patient Outcome Improvement National Tabulation System (POINTS), adjusts how annual fee schedule updates and budget neutrality thresholds are calculated, and changes data collection and reporting requirements for imaging services and clinical data registries.","summary_threshold":50,"title":"Patients First Act of 2026","viability":41.0,"viability_status":"active"},"date":"2026-07-15","detail":"Related bill","id":"119-hr-9693","label":"H.R. 9693","title":"Patients First Act of 2026","url":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/9693"}]},"selection":{"reason":"Impact scored and summarised by Gemini.","status":"analyzed"},"sources":{"latest":{"date":"2026-03-30","label":"Introduced in House","url":"https://www.congress.gov/119/bills/hr8163/BILLS-119hr8163ih.htm"},"other":[{"label":"This bill on Congress.gov","url":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/8163"},{"label":"CBO cost estimate: H.R. 8163, Provider Reimbursement Stability Act ","url":"https://www.cbo.gov/publication/62808"}],"past":[]},"sponsor":{"chamber":"House","district":"3","id":"M001210","name":"Rep. Murphy, Gregory F.","party":"R","state":"NC"},"stage":"committee_action","stage_label":"Committee hearing or markup","summary":"The Provider Reimbursement Stability Act of 2026 makes changes to the Medicare physician fee schedule to allow for larger annual adjustments, set rules for correcting differences between estimated and actual service utilization, require timely updates to direct cost data, and limit year-to-year variation in certain adjustment factors.","summary_detail":{"affected_groups":[{"effect":"Subject to revised annual payment adjustments, utilization reconciliation rules, and predictable limits on year-to-year fee schedule variations.","group":"Medicare healthcare providers and physicians"},{"effect":"Required to implement higher adjustment thresholds, conduct utilization difference calculations and reconciliations, update direct cost inputs every five years, and enforce a 2.5 percent limit on annual adjustment factor variations.","group":"Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS)"}],"key_provisions":["Increases the threshold for annual adjustments to the Medicare physician fee schedule from $20 million to $54.3 million starting in 2027.","Requires the CMS to index the budget neutrality threshold amount using the Medicare Economic Index (MEI) every five years starting in 2032.","Requires the CMS to compare estimated versus actual utilization for specific bundled or add-on services and adjust payments in a later year if the difference exceeds 0.1 percent of total estimated expenditures.","Mandates that the CMS update prices and rates for direct cost inputs (such as clinical staff wage rates, medical supplies, and equipment prices) simultaneously at least once every five years after consulting with stakeholders.","Prohibits the CMS from varying a specific budget neutrality adjustment factor by more than 2.5 percent each year starting in 2027."],"missing_information":["Exact long-term financial costs of raising the threshold to $54.3 million and indexing it to inflation.","Specific details on how the Secretary will determine appropriate methods for utilization reconciliation."],"plain_summary":"The Provider Reimbursement Stability Act of 2026 makes changes to the Medicare physician fee schedule to allow for larger annual adjustments, set rules for correcting differences between estimated and actual service utilization, require timely updates to direct cost data, and limit year-to-year variation in certain adjustment factors.","significance":"This legislation alters the mechanisms and numerical thresholds governing payment adjustments under the Medicare physician fee schedule, affecting how the federal government calculates and corrects provider reimbursement rates over time."},"summary_meta":{"generated_at":"2026-10-26T13:16:12+00:00","model":"gemini-3.5-flash-lite","source_basis":"text+summary","source_urls":["https://www.congress.gov/119/bills/hr8163/BILLS-119hr8163ih.htm"]},"summary_threshold":50,"title":"Provider Reimbursement Stability Act of 2026","viability":54.0,"viability_detail":{"breakdown":[{"available":true,"effective_weight":35.0,"inputs":[{"label":"Detected stage","value":"Committee hearing or markup"}],"key":"stage","label":"Legislative stage","note":"Stage value from the table on the About page.","points":14.0,"value":0.4,"weight":35},{"available":true,"effective_weight":15.0,"inputs":[{"label":"Committee activity","value":"Markup / ordered reported"}],"key":